#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407632 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e054f8-7842-51d9-944e-506f086c64ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407614} {:answer/id #uuid "30bd69d5-9aed-5f53-bd57-88c0d8b71e93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407615} {:answer/id #uuid "951b5e1a-06b7-5f54-b81b-2e7935528e41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407621} {:answer/id #uuid "b2537554-7ead-5776-b7ea-b0a5c6ea2d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407622} {:answer/id #uuid "de576d13-a320-57ea-81fe-65cb1f522e77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08e054f8-7842-51d9-944e-506f086c64ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407614} :answer-field/id #uuid "55f12593-79dd-5e1b-8574-a974a94bfc5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f48c368c-417f-5ffe-9791-056b549ac545" :question/math-academy-id "q-105559" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,i+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y\\,j+{e}^{2z}\\,k.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(0,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{2z}) \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y+\\pi y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y+2{e}^{2z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(0,1,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(0,1,0) & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =0+0-\\pi +2 \\\\  & =2-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408071 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3276117f-c9fb-5648-baa7-3ddf01111aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3276117f-c9fb-5648-baa7-3ddf01111aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408047} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c0686c8-e24f-53f9-b174-994346511b51" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40346cc1-7283-5552-9863-224be650a17a" :question/math-academy-id "q-275350" :question/problem "Let $r=⟨-4,-1,2⟩$, $q=⟨-2,3,1⟩$ and $p=⟨5,-2,-1⟩.$ Find $r\\cdot (q\\times p)$ using a determinant.\n\n$r\\cdot (q\\times p)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $r\\cdot (q\\times p)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $r,$ $q,$ and $p.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot (q\\times p) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-4)(3\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 1)+((-2)\\cdot (-1)-5\\cdot 1)+2((-2)\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =(-4)(-3+2)+(2-5)+2(4-15) \\\\  & =4-3-22 \\\\  & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56ef0874-008a-597f-b2cb-02be44cd3944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408103} {:answer/id #uuid "6d8dcecd-d942-5c7a-b18e-cb5a2602acc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408104} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf98593-9506-526d-9be6-5176a031c0ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408105} {:answer/id #uuid "a019b149-6685-5c94-9ead-07affcb89def" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408106} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d39875-cd66-5fd6-9485-32650cce5157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56ef0874-008a-597f-b2cb-02be44cd3944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408103} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fd432bd-eb14-5f9d-bc51-e98dfa9d5d17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "32b7570a-855d-5799-88e4-c24fe54119de" :question/math-academy-id "q-20053" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨-2,2,-3⟩$, $b=⟨2,-1,2⟩$ and $c=⟨-2,1,1⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2((-1)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 2)-2(2\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 2)-3(2\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =6-12+0 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408112 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a28f1b85-eac0-597c-848e-a2bdbc20cee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a28f1b85-eac0-597c-848e-a2bdbc20cee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408107} :answer-field/id #uuid "9284f3f7-2649-559e-ade1-b845ad584b38" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "627a3422-ad6a-5d23-b76b-6cf6cb390db2" :question/math-academy-id "q-317318" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨-1,2,4⟩$, $b=⟨0,3,1⟩$ and $c=⟨2,5,0⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using a determinant.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n\n$-15$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,-\\,2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,+\\,4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\,(3\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 5)\\,-\\,2\\,(0\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2)\\,+\\,4\\,(0\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =(-1)\\,(-5)\\,-\\,2\\,(-2)\\,+\\,4\\,(-6) \\\\  & =5+4-24 \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fd5ff55-9267-592c-8f53-54d27c28ddbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409591} {:answer/id #uuid "64ee45f8-90e8-584e-9ee0-f763b1794d3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2xy}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409596} {:answer/id #uuid "81f3041d-6679-5b2e-8990-3525e4b2226c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4xy}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409598} {:answer/id #uuid "9811b973-e6d5-574c-9870-5a2db507cfc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409600} {:answer/id #uuid "d498b33f-c852-5e73-bfc6-bc624cb1045e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4xy}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fd5ff55-9267-592c-8f53-54d27c28ddbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409591} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d405633-3d03-5b83-b9d8-c7d039eaf05b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a957b95-e78d-5791-8e30-44fc756d4a5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-131343" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j,\n$$ what is the scalar curl of $F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\right)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8286c6dd-7fea-5018-b722-69a0848b0bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437502} {:answer/id #uuid "5d52abdf-7ac5-55e3-92ae-9aec71a85bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8286c6dd-7fea-5018-b722-69a0848b0bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437502} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb53bf33-5552-5395-8fd4-4d42b50a2a01" :answer-field/key "$\\text{curl}\\,F=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438780} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4939bd9-e529-5af3-b533-59e09598d0e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437504} {:answer/id #uuid "2b9d89e5-98a9-5520-8de9-5ddb178c0664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b9d89e5-98a9-5520-8de9-5ddb178c0664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437505} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a596bab-34c8-5ee7-9e82-c47a2988e863" :answer-field/key "$\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438781} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "273314f4-9ed1-52ac-8d9b-6701a93d8748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437506} {:answer/id #uuid "265725b9-3d43-5f84-a380-552591b33792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "265725b9-3d43-5f84-a380-552591b33792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437507} :answer-field/id #uuid "05fd84cc-0dd3-5f03-9595-e86a0cb51c38" :answer-field/key "$\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438782} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fc09d16-5459-5df8-9d7e-c5304315ffbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437508} {:answer/id #uuid "3423417a-d67c-5bac-8ed6-720f61a5aa60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fc09d16-5459-5df8-9d7e-c5304315ffbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437508} :answer-field/id #uuid "31b1fd93-e1ee-539e-8481-cca67e61611b" :answer-field/key "$F$ is irrotational" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "656969c6-847a-533c-a9b8-d526f82e1ab3" :question/math-academy-id "q-105694" :question/problem "![](images/17/17637e938a25e91b9a03921dbbe0f22c8ea760f09cfed9b4a76d07bd5e3b9301.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](images/f3/f385add60afac473a5734f8c1ed57c6575bada03c9c16b24fed73b200c38e2c5.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$ is true, whereas statement $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate clockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement “$\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction” is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (clockwise), our thumb will point into the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points into the screen, in the direction of decreasing $z.$\n\n- Statement “$F$ is irrotational” is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$; “$\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29595116-806d-5a50-ab1b-9695013bab97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410234} {:answer/id #uuid "6cddf496-550e-503d-97cf-86f4c416cb41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410274} {:answer/id #uuid "87da3e47-9864-54d6-b40d-2913c6ae3373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410286} {:answer/id #uuid "ba4d127a-493d-5de0-a877-1ac772d55fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410329} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d42b31-2f13-5fd5-a529-f48c87f1b72a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cddf496-550e-503d-97cf-86f4c416cb41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410274} :answer-field/id #uuid "81bc68bf-9d8f-562a-bde6-27ff85778ca1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35202296-3c02-529f-93c9-ab75d2fe58b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-128355" :question/problem "Find $c\\cdot (a\\times b),$ if $b\\times c=⟨4,3,-5⟩$ and $a=⟨-2,8,2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-2,8,2⟩\\cdot ⟨4,3,-5⟩ \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 4+8\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-8+24-10 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (b\\times a)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (a\\times b)=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "402ff24c-ce68-5100-bc6f-ce08b50c1b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437378} {:answer/id #uuid "b55bf19b-e306-5cf0-a5ee-460b10f4d0dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b55bf19b-e306-5cf0-a5ee-460b10f4d0dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437379} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e0832f4-ea5d-510d-80b2-f2b601f5cea1" :answer-field/key "$F=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}\\,i-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{y}}\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438718} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65c63bc1-ce38-5b41-98d2-3546373c1d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437380} {:answer/id #uuid "aadf4a2f-a9fb-5a4e-a7c3-c50425746fba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65c63bc1-ce38-5b41-98d2-3546373c1d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437380} :answer-field/id #uuid "245451c8-c2d8-5660-b868-914d6a09643c" :answer-field/key "$G=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y\\,i-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438719} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee3af17a-9e86-50af-944e-c33c42e3d28e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437382} {:answer/id #uuid "c5abe579-6912-52ab-ae7a-530eb7394275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5abe579-6912-52ab-ae7a-530eb7394275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437383} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd21fc02-2d66-5119-b27a-c74692938a6c" :answer-field/key "$H$ $=(8x-3y)\\,i$ $+(4y-3x+1)\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b4c4358-44ac-586a-b065-3dc8b8db940a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104574" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}$ and $Q=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{y}}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y$ and $Q=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+1}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=8x-3y$ and $Q=4y-3x+1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle F=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}\\,i-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{y}}\\,j;\n$$ $H$ $=(8x-3y)\\,i$ $+(4y-3x+1)\\,j$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "519cca11-1af0-5c38-ac09-9cdf3e9eb8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439999} {:answer/id #uuid "6fbea3d5-d3a9-50cd-881a-7348e37c0dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fbea3d5-d3a9-50cd-881a-7348e37c0dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440000} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3485904-6c14-5182-a07e-0e8536e160b9" :answer-field/key "$F$ $=({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2})\\,i$ $+({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7})\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440357} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb20b90e-716b-5744-aa1c-1bc38cc09c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440001} {:answer/id #uuid "9e61d8e1-2d98-58e5-918c-f2fb0a227922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e61d8e1-2d98-58e5-918c-f2fb0a227922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440002} :answer-field/id #uuid "908db421-59c3-5473-9555-1804770c666a" :answer-field/key "$G=({y}^{3}-6x)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2})\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440358} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99e2896f-89e3-56ed-85e3-9569e0e553b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440003} {:answer/id #uuid "0b03afd1-5458-5b8f-80bb-6721de8fe5c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b03afd1-5458-5b8f-80bb-6721de8fe5c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440004} :answer-field/id #uuid "12bd3f31-bc54-5cfa-8930-506cacf26495" :answer-field/key "$H=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "953d7cc2-ab7b-52e3-b63e-6dc18089f06f" :question/math-academy-id "q-86896" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P={e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2}$ and $Q={e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P={y}^{3}-6x$ and $Q=3x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $Q=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F$ $=({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2})\\,i$ $+({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7})\\,j$; $G=({y}^{3}-6x)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2})\\,j$; $H=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,j$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411857 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "012fa540-5e42-507c-b4d7-4be584a8a37c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411681} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca03990-4e3c-545a-89cf-a853738bd60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411736} {:answer/id #uuid "74f3c49e-b5f3-5c5e-b0db-c523644cd561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411740} {:answer/id #uuid "d2fa903f-cfe5-50db-9712-2e6a3f05be62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411796} {:answer/id #uuid "de2d26a9-566a-53ef-8878-c12a27c0ec9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74f3c49e-b5f3-5c5e-b0db-c523644cd561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411740} :answer-field/id #uuid "f69e9c6d-9eea-5554-8872-d044f41b5762" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "47812564-3d50-5e9e-a030-d910fa667ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-161606" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}4x\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ for $x\\ge 0,$ $y\\ge 0$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=y+3.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/02/021047e8d472a91eeeb7b45d239a1577cb7b03c873e0cb7f20ddd5ca0b69b1a3.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=y+3=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z\\le r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}4x\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3}4{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [z]_{z=0}^{z=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +12{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +12{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +4{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}_{r=0}^{r=2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +4(2)^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +32\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +32\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-4(-1-1)+32(1-0)) \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411863 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "545b4aad-bc8c-59f4-a624-a099157d5076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411722} {:answer/id #uuid "8930c666-998a-5367-8dd0-b9dfcc9f93f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411751} {:answer/id #uuid "adfa686a-3a9f-5c43-baff-2feb8fd0100f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411769} {:answer/id #uuid "cb339401-80d8-5441-8a36-c4eb7250cbc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411789} {:answer/id #uuid "cfda085a-1d8b-55be-bd0f-4cfdeac71375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb339401-80d8-5441-8a36-c4eb7250cbc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411789} :answer-field/id #uuid "75ea2526-b966-5bc2-949f-e2b7d5c9deed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72611a88-9d34-5676-b505-ea5928bf6c8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-83500" :question/problem "![](images/55/55bbf6f55ce287321f2cc593ea48d65a78c655ca18d2a34df68b63c2bda92c8f.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the semicircle shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ))\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415272 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59393bce-26a0-53f4-b9ee-a4b60e0309ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453336} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ec25f8-9101-5ec4-ae2b-1947b4fed11b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3ec25f8-9101-5ec4-ae2b-1947b4fed11b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453337} :answer-field/id #uuid "6aafa3ab-4c0c-5ec8-8508-509f0aa15d9e" :answer-field/key "$F$ is conservative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454418} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59eb0512-2892-504c-817d-6e1c62f596b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453338} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2be482-8af1-5d7d-8d56-389be603fc9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c2be482-8af1-5d7d-8d56-389be603fc9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453339} :answer-field/id #uuid "359d2acf-2ea6-56b6-a467-a84c75cf4d9e" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=e,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454419} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7762d99b-6748-51f0-9769-d7e020b910e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453340} {:answer/id #uuid "761205e9-2c61-502f-8e9d-e4e3f37407aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7762d99b-6748-51f0-9769-d7e020b910e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453340} :answer-field/id #uuid "95713f76-4916-53c2-b271-58871420022e" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0027cfb5-d932-549f-8cc6-973642895e8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-125743" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=e,\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-{e}^{0}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =e\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is false. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$F$ is conservative”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=e,\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415273 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f497857-d2ea-515b-ac70-f83db9b8d663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415202} {:answer/id #uuid "394650d1-8e19-51ba-a71f-b0fc64e540e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415209} {:answer/id #uuid "88f09cc1-4f58-5190-94f6-808435cc1c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415228} {:answer/id #uuid "cdc0005d-5201-5c11-8909-40e11ed0a852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415248} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f0d68b-8c4e-509f-990d-cfc2226353ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0f0d68b-8c4e-509f-990d-cfc2226353ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415254} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5443ff1-90ef-5376-a9ca-4302c15cea52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13b80d0a-868f-5180-a5b8-148925e3a0e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-125756" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(2,0)$ to $(3,-2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(2,0)$ and $B(3,-2).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,-2)-f(2,0) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+1}-2\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01a6ae28-ab05-53de-9b3f-5e347ef15023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415194} {:answer/id #uuid "14b4d724-583b-5a37-a444-101bb4a57ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415199} {:answer/id #uuid "32debd00-39de-5d6e-9e96-d38fb37c9e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415208} {:answer/id #uuid "485effef-da1a-51e4-aa09-f8d8650ea3ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415214} {:answer/id #uuid "d067cbee-9b2c-5c36-8dc8-c47522a4af35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14b4d724-583b-5a37-a444-101bb4a57ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415199} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ad03362-7885-5567-9b35-4c8299722802" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c651ce1-2ea6-5480-b7a8-389903a354b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-125750" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+2y\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\,j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,1)$ to $(\\sqrt{3},2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-1,1)$ and $B(\\sqrt{3},2).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={y}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{3},2)-f(-1,1) \\\\  & =(2)^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}-(1)^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =4\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{19\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e94047db-68c4-551f-9095-b7004ca8440d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453324} {:answer/id #uuid "bb26d5f6-a66c-5844-b1b4-09be8fba0088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb26d5f6-a66c-5844-b1b4-09be8fba0088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453325} :answer-field/id #uuid "72a92e66-5e63-57a1-a0c4-007b140902d2" :answer-field/key "$F$ is conservative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454412} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90c212f9-6c73-5efa-827a-5c7e1827ff4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453326} {:answer/id #uuid "d032a76d-d4bb-5471-9f1d-f875814c51b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d032a76d-d4bb-5471-9f1d-f875814c51b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453327} :answer-field/id #uuid "7023734c-f76f-51a8-9faa-f0979cbea5ab" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2\\sqrt{2},$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454413} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09069a3c-ed3c-50a5-9d67-301f8b9abc91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453328} {:answer/id #uuid "da2b872b-a120-58a1-8c06-6c98a0c03cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09069a3c-ed3c-50a5-9d67-301f8b9abc91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453328} :answer-field/id #uuid "7125f222-da47-5c32-9e27-3e728ad73ef9" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-\\sqrt{2},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{2},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41c787c2-8fe3-51ae-aef3-bfbcd04677ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-125720" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨y,\\,x⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(\\sqrt{2},2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2\\sqrt{2},\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=xy.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{2},2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-\\sqrt{2},\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{2},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is false. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$F$ is conservative”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2\\sqrt{2},\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415276 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ed4975-6d31-5ec6-a093-5c910fbba055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415196} {:answer/id #uuid "3e05852b-fb2c-53ef-8b30-791b2b7932ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415210} {:answer/id #uuid "450c00f1-3112-5f7e-b618-7f3d64cf06cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415213} {:answer/id #uuid "c98cff8e-42c5-5b37-a1b7-f50921ee92cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415246} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3ee363-bc3b-5b3b-99ff-bea41f6d77d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c98cff8e-42c5-5b37-a1b7-f50921ee92cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415246} :answer-field/id #uuid "d67c0c93-91d0-53c7-a667-f86c44dece23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "975a843e-4bff-52d8-aa59-728678109c63" :question/math-academy-id "q-125747" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=6xy\\,i+3{x}^{2}\\,j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0)$ to $(2,-2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(1,0)$ and $B(2,-2).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=3{x}^{2}y.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,-2)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{2}\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09235254-4d47-5c19-a8cc-f2c8d86165e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415197} {:answer/id #uuid "43de946b-6276-5f5e-981d-3f9e49ef0b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415212} {:answer/id #uuid "991eb7ba-c62e-5f6d-b399-c7f8b3631d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415236} {:answer/id #uuid "c2db9fc1-1412-5d23-8af2-c825674e2420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-5e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415244} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b50554-1538-5e80-9bff-964961a87a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09235254-4d47-5c19-a8cc-f2c8d86165e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415197} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeed41f2-bb2a-5596-8fa5-7363fbf67f7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2675f4f-103b-59c5-b57c-1f769858716e" :question/math-academy-id "q-125759" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}\\,j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0)$ to $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(1,0)$ and $B(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(1,0) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi /2)}-1{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0} \\\\  & =2{e}^{1}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & =2e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61d2add2-3ad5-5382-bacd-16dbd27b4700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415219} {:answer/id #uuid "6d6c732d-930f-5808-b25d-6b951b402fff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415222} {:answer/id #uuid "7d41326b-ff6a-57f4-af5f-466b1c4babc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415226} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0f378c-b7c6-55ea-b7a0-f823f1d2518c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415230} {:answer/id #uuid "8bbbd95e-905c-5fbb-b3d2-7375a1f4547f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61d2add2-3ad5-5382-bacd-16dbd27b4700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415219} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cac645c-9b57-5ef6-9122-6b261e5ac034" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a75809b4-9c5b-5b68-8717-3a8614d8377f" :question/math-academy-id "q-125742" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=z{e}^{x+y}.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2,3,1)$ to $(0,1,-1)$ in ${R}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-2,3,1)$ and $B(0,1,-1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(0,1,-1)-f(-2,3,1) \\\\  & =(-1){e}^{0+1}-(1){e}^{-2+3} \\\\  & =-e-e \\\\  & =-2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b5add66-bcf6-5621-80cf-899ef43c417d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8-\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415198} {:answer/id #uuid "9549b62b-a175-541a-90f6-e4a181a146f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415234} {:answer/id #uuid "b76ff3bf-966d-5fca-9249-5a073557d751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415242} {:answer/id #uuid "dc9962c0-660e-54ff-aeb6-e838063c6839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415252} {:answer/id #uuid "edea968c-ca5f-5f22-97b5-05c3f197f4e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5-\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b5add66-bcf6-5621-80cf-899ef43c417d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8-\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415198} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c832baf-abd6-548a-b77a-513f27bf73e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd1e238f-7dc4-513f-b157-39e9312d6861" :question/math-academy-id "q-125741" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡z-y.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0,1)$ to $(2,-2,0)$ in ${R}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(1,0,1)$ and $B(2,-2,0).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(2,-2,0)-f(1,0,1) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-(-2))-(1\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1-0) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415280 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "260ed446-43fe-5beb-ab7d-78b6125c1cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5-e}{3e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415205} {:answer/id #uuid "50955a5e-a870-5833-935b-0d75056ca130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({e}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415215} {:answer/id #uuid "52fd34b1-d7f9-5a57-9656-43e29d60efa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-2{e}^{2}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415216} {:answer/id #uuid "99fc339c-7e1c-58ba-8069-a0ad53cdb041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415237} {:answer/id #uuid "a6959a00-ad55-53a1-8b0d-bd4bafcbb3fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3-2e}{2e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52fd34b1-d7f9-5a57-9656-43e29d60efa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-2{e}^{2}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415216} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a158f6e-8c5d-5eb1-9da0-cbf999c0636e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d068dd74-b0d6-5e4a-8ca9-75d15ab6e3ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-125733" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=y{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,2)$ to $(\\pi ,1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,2)$ and $B(\\pi ,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(\\pi ,1)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =(1){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }-(2){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1}-2e \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}-2e \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2{e}^{2}}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415281 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d72dfe19-644e-58ac-b092-ca0bf0a32235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453330} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3cb706-2c7c-59c6-9797-8f2413c3c0ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d3cb706-2c7c-59c6-9797-8f2413c3c0ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453331} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aa85dc7-977b-504c-9e63-6eaa9497ca86" :answer-field/key "$F$ is conservative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454415} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fc76864-a6b1-5bc1-9012-9c15cd8d9c39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453332} {:answer/id #uuid "9de221a3-7ed0-563b-8bfe-b20e6e8988e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9de221a3-7ed0-563b-8bfe-b20e6e8988e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453333} :answer-field/id #uuid "6454a9c4-5959-5c99-9e74-da908dab25c3" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=8,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x),$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454416} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fea26c04-19a4-5d02-95f7-713e29db1fb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453334} {:answer/id #uuid "05e0a248-34b8-5d04-9e61-a958287d7eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fea26c04-19a4-5d02-95f7-713e29db1fb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453334} :answer-field/id #uuid "771420e3-311e-5ce3-b340-03646c706117" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=3,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef46b0f0-6f4c-53dd-b0f1-5e1b301fe037" :question/math-academy-id "q-125736" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y},\\,x{e}^{y}⟩$ and the points $A(1,0)$ and $B(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=8,\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x),$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$” is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3-1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=3,\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$” is false. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$F$ is conservative”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=8,\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x),$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416109 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c51a5c2-f91d-5c41-ab32-882df0c70ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}(-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416077} {:answer/id #uuid "35da7698-acba-5a15-9327-eb06db3b24c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}(-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416078} {:answer/id #uuid "4b5c6d03-4f8a-5ba0-9230-947fbebb29b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}({r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416082} {:answer/id #uuid "89460c96-8227-5b1e-b670-b297b665d081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}(-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416088} {:answer/id #uuid "a650e27c-da32-56b0-88b1-5dd26bdd90fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}(-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35da7698-acba-5a15-9327-eb06db3b24c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}(-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416078} :answer-field/id #uuid "80955fcf-3708-5539-a929-c138089951ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06de88f2-295c-546b-af7c-8eedcb3a386b" :question/math-academy-id "q-126708" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e54e2dc5c314ed1dda6ef9c60db5c5df3058ba120afec42bddbabc2622c3ae98.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨x+y,\\,-xy⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(-xy)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e1/e1c053e1cc4b4304c1302da2c57278200931b84e4ba687269d0ea89a99b523b8.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x+y$ and $Q(x,y)=-xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(-xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 6,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}(-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cf2dceb-7c5d-5d9b-9403-414a88730866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416074} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5d7b5b-5c88-5245-85a2-eb913d758a87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{729\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416083} {:answer/id #uuid "96c867f3-7baa-5a33-8eba-e465e7463140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416090} {:answer/id #uuid "990e60bc-7dbd-5a34-81df-2112a3293326" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{243\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416092} {:answer/id #uuid "abee63b5-df6a-5f86-838c-404f4f8edb05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "990e60bc-7dbd-5a34-81df-2112a3293326" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{243\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416092} :answer-field/id #uuid "07315ac9-2ead-596d-85c1-f7d066447650" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "654eb25e-4dd2-5095-910d-a25787a1e455" :question/math-academy-id "q-126753" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{sin}^{3}⁡x-{y}^{3},{y}^{4}+{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({sin}^{3}⁡x-{y}^{3})\\,dx+({y}^{4}+{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e2/e218f4b6938b166aa6eacab02ce0276a4a863a0cfade8528816a54c2621b51e7.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={sin}^{3}⁡x-{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)={y}^{4}+{x}^{3},\n$$\n\n we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({sin}^{3}⁡x-{y}^{3})\\,dx+({y}^{4}+{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-(-3{y}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}3({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{81}{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{243}{4}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{243}{4}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =\\frac{243\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ff21cb-9e33-53f3-b627-a88a79716c30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416073} {:answer/id #uuid "3abf3164-1e19-59b8-a518-c866ee98eb86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416079} {:answer/id #uuid "7406e360-d054-59fe-8f8f-ff8531ac1adc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416086} {:answer/id #uuid "989f1950-08af-5b66-a148-ca15e1457577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416091} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b8980a-616f-5aa2-877a-5c0ee10376d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7406e360-d054-59fe-8f8f-ff8531ac1adc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416086} :answer-field/id #uuid "634f078a-6dcb-548c-8f80-dad54be93eec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d616e0c6-f6b2-5a83-83cf-32e007120166" :question/math-academy-id "q-126569" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨y(x-1),\\,x(y-1)⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}y(x-1)\\,dx+x(y-1)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/b9/b95320928a5e7482f9ba2b8bb1d1499950710545b4c7273b7f70dfe5ab8c84b8.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y(x-1)$ and $Q(x,y)=x(y-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y(x-1)\\,dx+x(y-1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}((y-1)-(x-1))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416112 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48ad61f7-0826-5aab-ae4e-a6c43c772071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416081} {:answer/id #uuid "5a99ee20-4856-5c51-8e4b-86d8dbf5a614" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416084} {:answer/id #uuid "a973df46-4a12-5e49-ab49-9f61bef424b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416097} {:answer/id #uuid "c06e1769-fccf-57f9-b3d1-36eb4847c17e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416101} {:answer/id #uuid "c3419ad6-a21f-5e70-a985-87306ae9808a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3419ad6-a21f-5e70-a985-87306ae9808a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416102} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4ef8539-57c5-5120-9995-c808fd171d2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdb21538-b802-59ae-83c3-94940f8f1068" :question/math-academy-id "q-126516" :question/problem "![](images/bc/bc0660c37a6658635311a9a973ae3f4802740622a0b35ade22d6050ce648efa6.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}({y}^{3}-x)\\,dx+({x}^{3}+{e}^{1/y})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/77/7724df0035511419dcb5117ba8aa337380325af22ee235d206a954aa12c62fe9.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{3}-x$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+{e}^{1/y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{3}-x)\\,dx+({x}^{3}+{e}^{1/y})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}3({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bbc76e1-b401-5a78-abad-35dac56a605e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453342} {:answer/id #uuid "279f518b-43b4-54ff-a27b-f222f36cb0f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "279f518b-43b4-54ff-a27b-f222f36cb0f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453343} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ab559b0-8ad8-5600-af4d-58237670463b" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne 0,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ in counterclockwise direction." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454421} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2848ae05-afe6-565a-9e41-aea348ad8825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453344} {:answer/id #uuid "b066d196-4087-59b9-80a0-674602ceaaf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b066d196-4087-59b9-80a0-674602ceaaf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453345} :answer-field/id #uuid "029fd954-167e-57fe-8991-0827db39c02c" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the segment line $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454422} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa9edb55-47b4-5ffa-8f84-e71046dcce7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453346} {:answer/id #uuid "9b304306-6e7d-5cf9-b636-c3306a1cc7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa9edb55-47b4-5ffa-8f84-e71046dcce7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453346} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eb5c926-ae08-5e20-a8c6-c74a02337973" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454423} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2382595d-1329-5487-addb-48a96fc302ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453348} {:answer/id #uuid "ccaebe90-5c00-5752-8300-47cb5c96cc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2382595d-1329-5487-addb-48a96fc302ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453348} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0ae2286-b965-58b3-9eee-07c2cde691c4" :answer-field/key "$F$ is conservative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b47df0de-b5ee-5562-b36a-a659cbe48d85" :question/math-academy-id "q-125796" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨-y,\\,x⟩$ and the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne 0,\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ in counterclockwise direction” is true. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi  \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the segment line $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$” is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨0,\\,1-2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨2t-1,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-2⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ is independent of path” is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419785 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aadbe221-860c-5e54-a0de-bd7fb0bef36e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aadbe221-860c-5e54-a0de-bd7fb0bef36e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419781} :answer-field/id #uuid "cea71914-4f17-5f64-8016-140e481b698d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20209d26-6af3-5176-8409-17df2aefc0b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-317313" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨1,-3,0⟩$, $b=⟨-2,2,0⟩$ and $c=⟨2,0,-4⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using a determinant.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix}\\,-\\,(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}\\,+\\,0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\,(2\\cdot (-4)-0\\cdot 0)\\,-\\,(-3)\\,((-2)\\cdot (-4)-0\\cdot 2)\\,+\\,0\\,((-2)\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =1\\,(-8)\\,-\\,(-3)\\,(8)\\,+\\,0\\,(-4) \\\\  & =-8+24+0 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "069decae-ea7d-53a6-9d7f-6cd1604d5e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "069decae-ea7d-53a6-9d7f-6cd1604d5e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419779} :answer-field/id #uuid "0082553f-6022-5a48-bc6b-791b1c483f07" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "944d3b7e-a470-59f8-92c4-a8abf2a8b72b" :question/math-academy-id "q-317329" :question/problem "Find $b\\cdot (c\\times a)$, if $-5a\\cdot ((6b)\\times (4c))=120.$\n\n$b\\cdot (c\\times a)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5a\\cdot ((6b)\\times (4c)) & =120 \\\\ (-5)\\cdot 6\\cdot 4\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =120 \\\\ (-120)[a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =120 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (a\\times c) & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (c\\times a) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419787 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f56b9f8-00ca-5814-abb9-6c5a7caba35e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f56b9f8-00ca-5814-abb9-6c5a7caba35e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419780} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dd14090-bb12-5afc-8250-d9460b7c0f27" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f15d30b5-fe4a-5207-9935-fd7503715554" :question/math-academy-id "q-317326" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨-2,0,-1⟩$, $b=⟨-3,-1,4⟩$ and $c=⟨5,-2,-2⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using a determinant.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\,-\\,0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\,+\\,(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\,((-1)(-2)-4\\cdot (-2))\\,-\\,0\\,((-3)\\cdot (-2)-4\\cdot 5)\\,+\\,(-1)\\,((-3)\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =(-2)\\,(10)\\,-\\,0\\,(-14)\\,+\\,(-1)\\,(11) \\\\  & =-20+0-11 \\\\  & =-31.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420996 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17573a89-2ada-5cfd-8592-1b89b18d61f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420990} {:answer/id #uuid "3856cad9-d2c1-55a4-adf6-784ae35a6aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420991} {:answer/id #uuid "5c54b913-d15a-53bc-b2c6-f71477530f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420992} {:answer/id #uuid "ae277bb1-236f-56f1-8a0c-7fd47295108c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420993} {:answer/id #uuid "d87585d5-b5cd-5fbc-b58c-a3d2c45f4e66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae277bb1-236f-56f1-8a0c-7fd47295108c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420993} :answer-field/id #uuid "f118ee24-10a3-5bda-8238-4be0365cace0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f22c7a35-af74-59c8-b71b-cae8347ea97d" :question/math-academy-id "q-37206" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+{z}^{2},$ where $x={s}^{2}+{t}^{2},$ $y=\\frac{s}{t}$ and $z=s{t}^{2}.$ Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{3{s}^{2}}{t}+{c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}st+{c}_{3}s{t}^{4},\n$$\n\nfind the value of ${c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{s}{t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={s}^{2}+{t}^{2}+s{t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{s}{t}+2s{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =2s, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =\\frac{1}{t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & ={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\left(\\frac{s}{t}\\right)\\cdot 2s+({s}^{2}+{t}^{2}+s{t}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+(\\frac{s}{t}+2s{t}^{2})\\cdot {t}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{s}^{2}}{t}+\\frac{{s}^{2}}{t}+t+st+st+2s{t}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{s}^{2}}{t}+t+2st+2s{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${c}_{1}=1,$ ${c}_{2}=2$ and ${c}_{3}=2,$ and therefore\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}=1+2+2=5.\n$$"}]}
